Rest in loops that wait by reading the clock.

Games wait for their next frame in loops that only call
System.currentTimeMillis(), keeping the processor busy at full speed. A
thread that reads the same millisecond 32 times in a row is such a loop, and
nothing it waits for can happen before the next millisecond. If no other Java
thread is ready to run, it sleeps until shortly before the clock reaches the
next millisecond, computed from the microseconds of the clock, and then reads
the clock again. The trigger counts reads and does not depend on how long a
read takes. A clock that does not advance within a millisecond gets a fixed
rest of half a millisecond instead.

